From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 15:13:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] feat(refinery): add a gc-native merge approval signal and
 fail-closed gate (gcp-dc8)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

gascity-packs keeps review BLOCKING, but the gc town has no producer of a
merge approval signal, so three merge-ready beads (gcp-xbl, gcp-g98, gcp-z2z)
have been parked with nothing able to release them. The gt-town refinery-merge
skill that leaked into this rig merges only on a formal GitHub review event
with state APPROVED, and nothing in gc emits one; gc's own review lane records
a verdict that is keyed to neither a PR nor a head SHA, so the refinery cannot
consume it either. This is a missing producer, not a missing town.

This commit adds both halves. record-merge-approval.sh is the producer: a
reviewer agent records verdict, PR number, head SHA, reviewer identity and
timestamp on the work bead in one bd update, so a half-written signal — a
verdict with no SHA to scope it — cannot exist. It refuses abbreviated SHAs,
unknown verdicts, unparseable PR references and anonymous approvals, writing
nothing on any of them.

checks/merge-approval-gate.sh is the consumer. It re-reads the LIVE PR head
from GitHub and permits a merge only when the approved SHA is still the head,
which is what makes the signal safe: an approval authorizes one commit, not a
branch, so commits pushed after review do not inherit it. Every other outcome
refuses, and the refusals split into two exit codes that never collapse into a
merge — exit 1 for a signal read and found wanting (missing, incomplete,
non-approving, wrong PR, stale SHA, PR closed or on another branch), exit 2 for
a verdict it could not reach at all (unreadable bead, PR lookup failure,
unresolvable head SHA). The opt-in switch itself fails closed: only recognized
off values disable the gate, so a typo in a rig's formula_vars enables review
rather than silently skipping it. A gh-less REST fallback mirrors the patrol
formula's own split, because a gate that cannot look up a PR would leave those
rigs permanently deadlocked — fail-closed, but exactly the failure this ends.

Validation: 15 test cases in gastown/tests/test_merge_approval_gate.sh, mostly
fail-closed coverage. Two red->green cycles confirmed the suite has teeth —
stubbing out the stale-SHA comparison, and making the opt-in fail open on an
unrecognized value, each turn the suite red. The REST path is exercised over a
hermetic PATH so the host's real gh cannot satisfy the lookup.

Formula wiring lands in the next commit.
